president's lawyer Rudy Giuliani says  the White House has a list of demands  for the special counsel before the  president will agree to sit down for an  interview with him Giuliani says the  president wants to testify in Robert  Muller's Russia investigation but  Giuliani is cautioning him against it  Paula Reid spoke with the former New  York Mayor she's outside the special  counsel's office in Washington Paula  what are you learning about the state of  negotiations  well Giuliani tells me that the special  counsels office has already rejected a  proposal to allow the president to  answer questions in writing so the two  sides will be negotiating the terms of a  possible in-person interview and  Giuliani tells me he wants the questions  in advance and there are certain  questions certainly issues that are  off-limits and despite my pressing he  would not specify exactly what those  issues are and there's also this  question about confidentiality and what  if anything in this interview would  eventually be made public and Giuliani  tells me that he wants some sort of  agreement about that in advance and the  opportunity to rebut anything that the  special counsels office puts out Rudy  Giuliani as the former US Attorney would  have been on the other side of the table  in these negotiations earlier in his  life Paula what do we know about when  this interview might take place if it  does exactly if it does Giuliani tells  me he would like to push it back past  the North Korea summit which is expected  sometime in the near future he tells me  he does not want to take the president  away from that important work for what  will likely be several days that he  would need to prepare him for something  like this all right we'll stay tuned  Paula thanks
 

